// Generated by `bun run docs:commands` from docs/ (see skill/sources.ts). Do not edit by hand.

export const SKILL_DOCS: Record<string, string> = {
  "generating.md": "# Generating models\n\n`zetta generate` makes a 3D model from a text prompt, one picture, or several views. `zetta splat create` makes Gaussian-splat objects and worlds.\n\nAll of them are paid commands: they check the price first and follow the [spend guard](#spending). Each one makes a new asset in your library. Every generation needs a paid plan; without one it fails with `PLAN_REQUIRED` (exit code 4). See [Your plan and billing](account.md#your-plan-and-billing).\n\n## Inputs\n\n| Input | Flag | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Text (up to 1,024 characters) | `-p, --prompt` | `-p \"a stylised wooden treasure chest\"` |\n| One picture | `-i, --image` | `-i chair.png`, `-i https://…/chair.jpg`, `-i image:812` |\n| Several views | `--view slot=picture` (repeat) | `--view front=f.png --view back=b.png` |\n\n**Pictures.** A picture can be a local file (PNG, JPEG or WebP, each side 128–5000 px, at most 8 MB), an `https://` URL, or `image:<id>` from your [image library](images.md). Local files are checked and uploaded for you.\n\n**View slots.** `front`, `back`, `left` and `right`.\n- **Required:** a `front` view and at least one other, on every engine.\n- **Missing views:** `--draw-missing` draws the missing `front`, `back`, `left` and `right` views with AI from the one you gave.\n\n**Text.** No engine builds from a bare prompt. `zetta` first makes a picture from your prompt with the image engine, then builds the model from that picture. The picture is made only after you approve the spend.\n\n**One input at a time.** Give `--prompt`, `--image` or `--view`. A prompt doesn't go with a picture or with views.\n\n## Engines\n\n| `--engine` | Inputs | Quality levels | Texture | Polygons |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `n2.5` (default) | text, picture, views (front + 1) | lowpoly, high, ultra | standard, detailed | low-poly 1,000–20,000; high up to 1M; ultra up to 2M. Quads 500–10,000 on low-poly, up to 150,000 otherwise. `--parts` |\n| `np1` | text, picture, views (front + 1) | — | standard, detailed, extreme | 48–20,000 triangles |\n| `np1.5` | text, picture, views (front + 1) | — | standard, detailed, extreme | 48–50,000 triangles, or 48–25,000 quads |\n\nEvery engine needs a paid plan. `zetta catalog engines --json` has the exact limits, including the face-count range and default for each quality level and polygon type. `zetta catalog entitlements` shows what your plan allows: `threeD.generate` is `false` without a plan, and `threeD.engines` lists `n25`, `np1` and `np15`.\n\nN1 is retired: `--engine n1` is refused, and a saved `defaults.engine n1` fails with a message saying so. Use `n2.5` or `np1`.\n\n## Options\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--quality lowpoly\\|high\\|ultra` | N2.5 only: low-poly, high-res (default) or ultra detail (a paid extra) |\n| `--no-texture` | Geometry only (cheaper) |\n| `--pbr` / `--no-pbr` | PBR materials; on by default for textured models |\n| `--texture-quality standard\\|detailed\\|extreme` | Texture detail. `extreme` is 8K, on np1 and np1.5 only (`8k` is accepted too) |\n| `--faces N` | Polygon budget, within the engine's range for that quality. Defaults to the most allowed; another count is a paid extra |\n| `--quads` | Quad topology instead of triangles (N2.5 and np1.5) |\n| `--parts` | Split into separate editable parts (N2.5, geometry only, not low-poly) |\n| `--texture-alignment image\\|geometry` | Fit the texture to the picture, or to the mesh (default) |\n| `--auto-size` | Scale the model to real-world size |\n| `--delight` | Remove baked-in lighting from the picture before texturing |\n| `--autofix` | Clean up the picture before building (slower) |\n| `--compress` | Smaller files with geometry compression |\n| `--no-uv` | Skip UV unwrapping (faster, smaller) |\n| `--fast-texture` | Faster standard-quality texturing |\n| `--seed N`, `--texture-seed N` | Reproducible results |\n| `--remove-bg` | Remove the picture's background first (good for product photos) |\n| `--draw-missing` | With `--view`: draw the missing side views with AI |\n| `--title`, `--tag` (repeat), `--visibility public\\|private` | Library details (private needs a paid plan) |\n\nOptions that don't work together are rejected **before** anything is uploaded or charged, with a message saying what to change (exit code 2). Options your plan doesn't allow are rejected the same way (exit code 4), and so is any generation without a plan (`PLAN_REQUIRED`).\n\n## Spending\n\n| Flag | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| `--dry-run` | Show the price and stop. Nothing is uploaded or spent |\n| `--max-credits N` | Spend only if the price is at most N |\n| `--yes` | Spend without asking |\n| *(neither, in a terminal)* | You are asked to confirm |\n| *(neither, no terminal)* | Refused with `SPEND_NOT_CONFIRMED`, exit code 5. Nothing is spent |\n| `--idempotency-key K` | Reusing the same key never charges twice; use it to retry safely |\n\nSet `zetta config set spend.maxPerCommand 200` to let scripts spend up to 200 credits per command without `--yes`. Failed jobs are refunded automatically.\n\n## Waiting and downloading\n\n| Flag | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| *(none, in a terminal)* | Waits, then downloads the result files into the current folder ([automatic downloads](configuration.md#automatic-downloads)) |\n| *(none, scripts and agents)* | Returns at once: `{\"id\": 1234, \"status\": \"queued\", …, \"next\": \"zetta asset wait 1234\"}` |\n| `--wait` | Waits for the job to finish |\n| `--timeout 10m` | Stops waiting after this long. Exit code 10; the job keeps running |\n| `--out <folder>` | Waits, then downloads the result files there |\n| `--download` / `--no-download` | Turns the automatic download on or off for this command |\n| `--no-wait` | Returns at once, without downloading |\n| `--format glb` | When downloading: only these formats |\n\n`zetta config set download.auto false` turns automatic downloads off, and `true` turns them on for scripts too. `zetta config set defaults.wait true` waits by default without downloading (`--no-wait` overrides it).\n\n## Examples\n\n```bash\n# Text → a low-poly model\nzetta generate -p \"a low-poly fox, stylised\" --quality lowpoly --yes --wait\n\n# A product photo → clean textured model, downloaded as GLB and FBX\nzetta generate -i shoe.jpg --remove-bg --engine n2.5 --max-credits 120 --out ./out --format glb --format fbx\n\n# Four views, with the missing back drawn by AI\nzetta generate --view front=f.png --view left=l.png --view right=r.png --draw-missing --engine n2.5 --yes\n\n# Game-ready: quads at a fixed budget\nzetta generate -i robot.png --engine n2.5 --quality lowpoly --quads --faces 8000 --max-credits 100 --wait\n\n# Highest texture detail\nzetta generate -i vase.jpg --engine np1.5 --texture-quality extreme --max-credits 200 --out .\n\n# Everything as JSON input (handy for scripts and agents)\nzetta generate --input '{\"prompt\":\"a ceramic mug\",\"engine\":\"n2.5\",\"maxCredits\":60}'\n```\n\n## Splat worlds (early access)\n\n`zetta splat create` makes Gaussian-splat objects and environments. It needs early access: ask for it with `zetta account early-access \"Zetta Splat\"`. Splats, world exports and video uploads don't work with an API key: they are refused with `SPLAT_NOT_IN_API`.\n\n| `--engine` | Makes | From |\n|---|---|---|\n| `splat` (default) | An object or a scene | One picture, or a prompt |\n| `splat2` | A whole world | A picture, a video, or several pictures of the same place |\n| `splat2.1` | A whole world (newer) | Same as `splat2` |\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `-i, --image <picture>` | The main picture |\n| `--video <file or URL>` | A walkthrough video (MP4, WebM, MOV or AVI, up to 100 MB; `splat2`/`splat2.1`). A video builds a place, so it needs subject `environment` |\n| `-p, --prompt \"…\"` | Describes the scene, up to 1,024 characters. Without a picture or video, a picture is made from it first |\n| `--subject object\\|environment` | What the scene is. Default: `environment` on `splat2`/`splat2.1`, `object` on `splat` |\n| `--aspect-ratio 16:9\\|21:9\\|3:2\\|4:3\\|1:1` | With `--prompt` alone: the frame of the environment picture made from it (default `16:9`) |\n| `--resolution 1K\\|2K\\|4K` | With `--prompt` alone: the size of the environment picture made from it (default `1K`) |\n| `--remove-bg` | Cut the object out of its background first (an object, on the `splat` engine only) |\n| `--world-view <azimuth>=<picture>` | More pictures of the same place, by direction in degrees (1–359) clockwise from the main picture. Up to 3, or 7 with `--same-space` |\n| `--same-space` | Reconstruct from all the pictures together (up to 8) |\n| `--seed N` | Reproducible results (`splat` engine) |\n\n`--aspect-ratio` and `--resolution` shape only an environment picture made from a prompt. Beside `--image` or `--video`, or with subject `object`, they are refused.\n\n```bash\nzetta splat create --image room.jpg --engine splat2 --dry-run\nzetta splat create --video walkthrough.mp4 --engine splat2.1 --yes --wait\nzetta splat create --image lobby.jpg --world-view 90=right.jpg --world-view 180=back.jpg --engine splat2 --yes\nzetta splat create -p \"a misty pine forest clearing\" --engine splat2 --aspect-ratio 21:9 --resolution 2K --dry-run\n```\n\n**Exports from a world:**\n\n```bash\nzetta splat export 1300 --as splat --resolution 500k --yes --out ./world   # a PLY splat (full_res, 500k, 150k or 100k)\nzetta splat export 1300 --as mesh --mesh-variant textured --yes --out .     # a mesh (textured or vertex_colored)\nzetta splat export 1300 --as collider --yes --out ./unity/Assets           # a light collider mesh for physics\n```\n",
  "refining.md": "# Refining models\n\nThe finishing tools work on any model:\n- one from your library, given by its asset id;\n- a local `.glb` file;\n- a URL to a `.glb`, `.obj` or `.fbx`.\n\nEach run makes a **new asset** and leaves the original untouched. They are paid commands, with the same flags as `generate`: `--dry-run`, `--max-credits`, `--yes`, `--idempotency-key`, `--wait`, `--timeout`, `--out`, `--format`, `--title`, `--tag` and `--visibility`. Finishing tools need a paid plan.\n\n```bash\nzetta refine <tool> <asset-id | file.glb | url> [options]\n```\n\n## Retopology: `zetta refine retopo`\n\nRebuilds the mesh as clean topology at a lower polygon count. It runs on Zetta R1, to an exact budget.\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--faces N` | 500–20,000 triangles, or 500–10,000 quads (default 8,000) |\n| `--quads` | Quad topology |\n| `--no-bake` | Bare geometry. By default the textures are carried onto the new mesh |\n| `--unwrap-uvs` | Unwrap fresh UVs in the same job, at no extra cost (replaces the UV layout) |\n| `--part <name>` | Only these parts of a segmented model |\n\n```bash\nzetta refine retopo 1234 --faces 5000 --quads --yes --wait\nzetta refine retopo ./scan.glb --faces 2000 --unwrap-uvs --dry-run\n```\n\n## Segmentation: `zetta refine segment`\n\nSplits a model into separate parts.\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--smart` | Smarter segmentation (better parts, costs more) |\n| `--granularity simple\\|balanced\\|detailed` | How finely to split |\n| `--no-split-by-connectivity` | Keep disconnected pieces together |\n| `--prompt \"seat, legs, backrest\"` | With `--smart`: which parts you want |\n\n## Smart UV: `zetta refine uv`\n\nUnwraps clean UVs, for models up to 80,000 triangles.\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--bake` | Bake the existing texture onto the new UVs. Off by default: you get bare UVs |\n| `--orientation as-is\\|+x\\|-x\\|+y\\|-y` | Which way the model faces in the export (default `as-is`) |\n| `--compress` | Compress geometry |\n\n## Texturing: `zetta refine texture`\n\nRepaints the texture from a reference picture, a description, both, or pictures of several sides.\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--reference <picture>` | A file, URL or `image:<id>` showing the look you want |\n| `--prompt \"…\"` | A description. It is turned into a reference picture first (needs image editing on your plan) |\n| `--view side=picture` (repeat) | Reference pictures of several sides instead: `front` (required), `back`, `left`, `right`. Not with `--reference` or `--prompt` |\n| `--tier standard\\|hd\\|8k` | Texture resolution: standard (2K), hd (4K) or 8k, each at its own price |\n| `--alignment image\\|geometry` | Follow the picture (default) or the mesh |\n| `--pbr` / `--no-pbr` | PBR materials (on by default) |\n| `--no-bake` | Don't bake material effects into the base texture (baked by default) |\n| `--no-delight` | Keep the lighting in the reference (by default it is removed first) |\n| `--fast` | The faster setting of the standard tier |\n| `--remove-bg` | Remove the reference pictures' backgrounds first |\n| `--part <name>`, `--seed N` | Only these parts; reproducible results |\n\n```bash\nzetta refine texture 1234 --reference oak.jpg --tier hd --yes --wait\nzetta refine texture 1234 --prompt \"brushed steel with scratches\" --dry-run\nzetta refine texture 1234 --view front=f.png --view back=b.png --dry-run\n```\n\n## Rigging: `zetta refine rig`\n\nAdds a skeleton so the model can be animated.\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--rig-type biped\\|quadruped\\|hexapod\\|octopod\\|avian\\|serpentine\\|aquatic\\|others` | Body plan (default `biped`). `others` is for a creature none of the others describe |\n| `--rig-spec standard\\|mixamo\\|unity\\|unreal\\|vrm\\|actorcore` | Bone naming, for a biped only: standard (default), mixamo, unity (Humanoid), unreal (UE5 Mannequin), vrm (VRM 1.0) or actorcore |\n| `--rig-format glb\\|fbx` | File format of the rigged model (default `glb`) |\n\nNot sure which body plan? `zetta asset rig-check <id>` looks at the model and suggests one (for example `quadruped`, with a reason). Add 1–4 `--frame <picture>` flags to give it your own renders, from different angles, instead of the thumbnail. Rig checks need a paid plan.\n\n```bash\nzetta asset rig-check 1234\nzetta asset rig-check 1234 --frame front.png --frame side.png --frame back.png\nzetta refine rig 1234 --rig-type quadruped --rig-format fbx --yes --out ./models\nzetta refine rig 1234 --rig-spec unreal --dry-run\n```\n\n## Conversion\n\n### Free, on your machine: `zetta asset export`\n\nConverts a GLB without using the server or spending credits.\n\n| `--format` | Output |\n|---|---|\n| `obj` | `.obj` + `.mtl` + the textures as files |\n| `fbx` | Binary `.fbx` with the textures embedded, and also written beside it. `--fbx-preset unity\\|unreal\\|blender\\|maya\\|threejs` sets axes and units (default `unity`) |\n| `stl` | Binary `.stl` (geometry only, for 3D printing) |\n\nSome details:\n- **Input:** an asset id (its GLB is fetched) or a local `.glb`.\n- **Geometry:** node transforms are baked into it.\n- **Compressed GLBs:** models with compressed geometry are refused; use the server conversion for those.\n\n```bash\nzetta asset export 1234 --format fbx --fbx-preset blender --out ./blender\nzetta asset export ./chair.glb --format stl --out ./print\n```\n\n### On the server: `zetta refine convert`\n\nFor USDZ, 3MF and glTF, and for clean-up while converting. Options that change geometry or textures switch to the advanced, pricier tier.\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--to gltf\\|fbx\\|usdz\\|obj\\|stl\\|3mf` | Target format (any case) |\n| `--quad`, `--symmetry`, `--faces N` | Quad topology (FBX); forced symmetry (with `--quad`); a face limit of 1,000–1,000,000, or 1,000–150,000 with `--quad` |\n| `--flatten-bottom`, `--flatten-threshold X` | Flatten the base, for printing or placing |\n| `--texture-size 128–4096`, `--texture-format jpeg\\|png\\|webp\\|…` | Texture output |\n| `--pack-uv`, `--vertex-colors` | Pack UVs; export vertex colours |\n| `--pivot-bottom`, `--scale X`, `--orientation +x\\|-x\\|+y\\|-y` | Pivot at the bottom centre, scale, forward axis |\n| `--with-animation`, `--in-place`, `--fbx-preset blender\\|3dsmax\\|mixamo` | Animation and FBX target |\n\n```bash\nzetta refine convert 1234 --to usdz --yes --out ./ar\n```\n\n## Chaining\n\nEach step returns the new asset's id, so tools chain naturally:\n\n```bash\nid=$(zetta generate -i robot.png --engine n2.5 --max-credits 100 --wait --json | jq -r .id)\ntopo=$(zetta refine retopo \"$id\" --faces 6000 --max-credits 60 --wait --json | jq -r .id)\nzetta refine rig \"$topo\" --rig-type biped --rig-format fbx --max-credits 60 --out ./out\n```\n\nFor the same chain over many inputs, use a [pipeline](pipelines.md).\n",
  "pipelines.md": "# Pipelines\n\nA **pipeline** is a saved recipe of steps, for example: picture → remove background → make a model → retopology → rig. Each **run** starts from one seed (a picture, pictures of each side, or a model) and runs the steps on Zetta's servers. **Batches** start one run per seed: a folder of product photos becomes a folder of rigged models.\n\nPipelines are the same ones as in the web editor (`/pipelines`), so a recipe made in either place opens in the other.\n\n## Recipes\n\nRecipes are YAML (or JSON) files. Steps have ids; `from:` wires them together.\n\n```yaml\nversion: 1\ntitle: Picture to rigged character\nsteps:\n  photo:  { kind: seed_picture }\n  clean:  { kind: remove_background, from: photo }\n  model:  { kind: generate_3d, from: clean, engine: n25, quality: high, output: textured }\n  topo:   { kind: retopology, from: model, engine: r1, faces: 10000 }\n  rig:    { kind: rigging, from: topo, rigType: biped, rigSpec: standard, format: fbx }\n```\n\nHow the format works:\n- **`from:`** names another step. Use `step.port` for a specific output, e.g. `split.each`, or a list when a step takes several inputs.\n- **Settings** use the friendly names below. Anything you leave out takes the web editor's default.\n- **`layout:`** is optional (positions on the canvas). Without it, steps are laid out left to right.\n\nRun `zetta pipeline schema` to print four complete example recipes. `zetta pipeline schema --json` gives the JSON Schema and every step kind with its settings.\n\n### Steps\n\n| Kind | Takes | Gives | Settings |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `seed_picture` | — | picture | *(the picture is given per run)* |\n| `seed_model` | — | mesh | *(the model is given per run)* |\n| `seed_views` | — | views | *(pictures of each side are given per run; front required)* |\n| `remove_background` | picture | picture | — |\n| `ai_edit` | picture | picture | `prompt`, `askAtRun` (ask for the instruction on each run), `for3D` |\n| `draw_views` | picture | views | `views`: `back`, `left`, `right` |\n| `isolate_parts` | picture | `character` and `each` (one branch per part) | `identify`, `sensitivity: low\\|medium\\|high`, `maxParts`, `pose: t\\|a`, `extraParts` |\n| `generate_3d` | one picture | mesh | `engine: n25`, `quality: low\\|high\\|ultra`, `output: textured\\|geometry`, `pbr`, `polygonCount`, `polygonType: triangle\\|quad`, `textureQuality: standard\\|detailed`, `segmentParts`, `realWorldScale`, `textureAlignment: mesh\\|picture`, `delight`, `autofixImage`, `compressGeometry`, `exportUv`, `removeBackground`, `availability`, `title` |\n| `generate_smart_3d` | one picture | mesh | as `generate_3d`, with `engine: np1\\|np15` and `textureQuality: …\\|extreme` |\n| `generate_3d_multiview` | views | mesh | as `generate_3d`; takes views from `seed_views` or `draw_views` |\n| `generate_smart_3d_multiview` | views | mesh | as `generate_smart_3d`; takes views |\n| `retopology` | mesh | mesh | `faces`, `polygonType`, `bake`, `unwrapUvs` (always the R1 engine) |\n| `segmentation` | mesh | mesh | `mode: segment\\|smart`, `granularity: simple\\|balanced\\|detailed`, `splitByConnectivity`, `hint` |\n| `texture` | mesh (+ optional reference picture) | mesh | `tier: standard\\|detailed\\|8k`, `prompt`, `reference`, `removeReferenceBackground`, `alignment`, `bake`, `pbr`, `fast`, `delight` |\n| `texture_multiview` | mesh (+ optional views) | mesh | as `texture`, with `views: {front, back?, left?, right?}` instead of `prompt`/`reference`. Without wired views or a `views` setting, each run asks for the pictures |\n| `smart_uv` | mesh | mesh | `bake`, `orientation: as-is\\|+x\\|-x\\|+y\\|-y`, `compress` |\n| `rigging` | mesh | mesh | `rigType: biped\\|quadruped\\|avian\\|serpentine\\|aquatic\\|hexapod\\|octopod\\|others`, `rigSpec: standard\\|mixamo\\|unity\\|unreal\\|vrm\\|actorcore` (humanoids only), `format: glb\\|fbx` |\n\nZetta N1 is retired. Recipes that still name it load with a warning; change the step to `n25`. Older recipes that feed views into `generate_3d` or `generate_smart_3d` are read as the multi-view step, with a warning.\n\nA recipe that starts from pictures of each side:\n\n```yaml\nversion: 1\ntitle: Product from photos of each side\nsteps:\n  photos: { kind: seed_views }\n  model:  { kind: generate_smart_3d_multiview, from: photos, engine: np15, polygonCount: 12000 }\n  paint:  { kind: texture_multiview, from: { in: model, views: photos }, tier: detailed }\n  uv:     { kind: smart_uv, from: paint }\n```\n\n## Describe a recipe\n\nAn AI can draft a recipe from a description, or change an existing one. It uses AI tokens and needs a paid plan (at most 10 a minute). Nothing is saved unless you ask:\n\n```bash\nzetta pipeline draft \"a game-ready character from a photo, rigged for Unity\" --output text > recipe.yaml\nzetta pipeline draft \"use quads everywhere\" --from recipe.yaml --output text > recipe-quads.yaml\nzetta pipeline draft \"add AI texturing at the end\" --update 12      # changes pipeline 12\nzetta pipeline draft \"products from photos of each side\" --create   # saves a new pipeline\n```\n\nThe printed recipe lists the draft's title, problems and per-run inputs as `#` comments, so it can be saved straight to a file and checked with `zetta pipeline validate`.\n\n## Create, check and price\n\n```bash\nzetta pipeline validate recipe.yaml              # checks the recipe here and on the server; free\nzetta pipeline estimate recipe.yaml --seeds 20   # price per run, and for 20 runs\nzetta pipeline create --file recipe.yaml         # saves it → pipeline id\nzetta pipeline list\nzetta pipeline get 12 --output text > recipe.yaml   # back to an editable recipe\nzetta pipeline update 12 --file recipe.yaml\nzetta pipeline duplicate 12\nzetta pipeline delete 12 --yes\n```\n\nPipelines made in the web editor come back as recipes too, so you can edit them in a text editor and save them back.\n\n## Run\n\n```bash\nzetta pipeline run 12 ./shoe.jpg --max-credits 300            # one run\nzetta pipeline batch 12 ./photos --max-credits 3000           # one run per picture in the folder\nzetta pipeline batch 12 a.jpg b.jpg image:812 --yes           # files, library pictures…\nzetta pipeline batch 12 1234 1235 --yes                       # …or library models, for seed_model pipelines\n```\n\nFor a pipeline that starts from pictures of each side (`seed_views`), each run is a set of pictures, front required:\n\n```bash\nzetta pipeline run 14 --views front=f.png,back=b.png,left=l.png --max-credits 300\nzetta pipeline batch 14 --views front=a1.png,back=a2.png --views front=b1.png --yes   # each front= starts a new run\nzetta pipeline batch 14 ./products --max-credits 3000    # products/chair/front.png, products/chair/back.png, …\n```\n\nA folder that holds `front.png` (plus `back`, `left`, `right`) is one run, named after the folder. A folder of such folders gives one run each. `image:<id>` works for any side.\n\nHow runs are started:\n- **Total price first:** the whole batch is priced and checked against `--max-credits` before anything starts. Steps that fan out (Isolate Parts) cost more for each part found, so the price shown is a minimum.\n- **Large batches:** more than 20 seeds are sent in several batches.\n- **`--mode manual`:** each step waits for you (`zetta run continue`).\n- **API keys:** a run uses the pipeline as it is saved. The CLI sends its own copy of the recipe only when it differs, which an API key is not allowed to do (`PIPELINE_EDIT_NOT_ALLOWED`); it then warns and runs the saved recipe. A key also cannot draft, create, edit, duplicate or delete pipelines, or run a recipe with a Gaussian-splat step (`SPLAT_NOT_IN_API`).\n- **Paid plan:** runs, batches and drafts need one (`PLAN_REQUIRED`).\n- **`--draft`:** creates the runs without starting them, so you can set per-run inputs first. For example, an AI texturing prompt:\n\n```bash\nzetta pipeline batch 12 ./photos --draft\nzetta run set-input 88 paint --prompt \"matte black ceramic\"\nzetta run set-input 88 paint --reference ./swatch.jpg\nzetta run set-input 88 paint --views front=f.png,back=b.png   # Multi-view - AI texturing\nzetta run start 88 --max-credits 200\n```\n\n## Follow and control runs\n\n```bash\nzetta run get 88                     # every step: status, asset id, error\nzetta run wait 88 --timeout 5m       # exit 0 when done or waiting on you · 9 failed · 10 still running\nzetta run wait 88                    # in a terminal: wait, then save the final models here\nzetta run wait 88 --out ./out        # wait, then download every model it made\nzetta run outputs 88 --out ./out --final-only     # only the last step of each branch\nzetta pipeline runs 12               # recent runs of a pipeline\n```\n\nWhen a run waits on you, `zetta run get` / `wait` list what is blocking it, with the exact next command:\n\n| Waiting for | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Isolate Parts review | `zetta run confirm-parts 88 <stepId> --keys head,torso` |\n| A manual-mode step | `zetta run step-run 88 <stepId>` or `zetta run continue 88` |\n| A failed step (refunded) | `zetta run step-retry 88 <stepId> [--set quality=ultra]` |\n| A draft | `zetta run set-input …`, then `zetta run start 88` |\n| A pause | `zetta run resume 88` |\n\nOther controls: `zetta run pause 88`, `zetta run cancel 88` (steps not yet started are not charged), `zetta run mode 88 auto|manual`, `zetta run delete 88 --yes` (only a run that is done, failed, cancelled or still a draft; the models stay in your library).\n",
  "library.md": "# Your library\n\nEvery model you generate, refine or import is an **asset** with a numeric id. Pictures live in a separate [image library](images.md), referenced as `image:<id>`.\n\n## Listing and finding\n\n```bash\nzetta asset list                                     # newest first, 24 per page\nzetta asset list --limit 10 --page 2\nzetta asset list --status processing --status queued\nzetta asset list --kind autoRigging                  # one kind only (see below)\nzetta asset list --search chair --visibility public  # search matches ids, titles and tags\nzetta asset list --group-runs --sort updated         # one entry per pipeline run; last changed first\nzetta asset list --oldest-first --json\n```\n\nThe kinds are `img23d`, `txt23d`, `multiView23d`, `retopology`, `segmentation`, `smartSegmentation`, `uvUnwrap`, `retexture`, `autoRigging`, `convertFormat`, `upload`, `imageToSplat`, `worldSplatExport` and `worldMeshExport`. `--sort` is `created` (default) or `updated`.\n\nEach item comes back in the same compact shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": 1234, \"status\": \"success\", \"kind\": \"img23d\", \"engine\": \"n25\", \"engineLabel\": \"Zetta N2.5\",\n  \"title\": \"Lounge chair\", \"tags\": [\"furniture\"], \"availability\": \"private\", \"prompt\": null, \"error\": null,\n  \"files\": [{ \"key\": \"…/model.glb\", \"format\": \"glb\", \"bytes\": 7447908, \"label\": null }],\n  \"thumbnailUrl\": \"https://…\", \"pipelineRunId\": null, \"createdAt\": \"…\", \"updatedAt\": \"…\"\n}\n```\n\nWith `--group-runs`, each pipeline run is one item: its newest model, with a `run` field that holds the run's `id`, `pipelineId`, `pipeline` title, `count` and the ids of every model it made (`members`).\n\n## Status, waiting, downloading\n\n```bash\nzetta asset get 1234                               # status, queue position, files\nzetta asset wait 1234 --timeout 5m                 # exit 0 success · 9 failed/cancelled · 10 still running\nzetta asset wait 1234 --out ./models --format glb  # wait, then download\nzetta asset wait 1234 --no-download                 # only wait (in a terminal it downloads here by default)\nzetta asset download 1234 --out ./models           # every result file\nzetta asset download 1234 --format fbx --format usdz\nzetta asset download 1234 --primary                # just the best model file (GLB, then FBX, glTF, OBJ)\nzetta asset download 1234 --overwrite              # replace files instead of adding -2, -3 …\n```\n\nHow downloads work:\n- **Links:** each download uses a fresh pre-signed link. The CLI fetches it without sending your token.\n- **File names:** files are saved as `<id>-<original name>`.\n- **Statuses:** `pending → queued → processing → saving → success | failed | cancelled`. Failed jobs are refunded automatically, and `zetta asset get` shows why the job failed.\n\n## Organising and sharing\n\n```bash\nzetta asset rename 1234 \"Lounge chair\"\nzetta asset tag 1234 --add furniture --add hero      # --remove x, or --set a,b to replace all\nzetta asset auto-tag 1234 --apply                    # suggests a category and tags with AI (paid plans; uses AI tokens)\nzetta asset share 1234                               # public; prints the link\nzetta asset unshare 1234                             # private again (paid plans)\nzetta asset get 17 --public                          # read anyone's shared asset, no sign-in needed\nzetta asset featured                                 # community highlights\n```\n\n## Cancelling and deleting\n\n```bash\nzetta asset cancel 1240              # only before the job starts; full refund\nzetta asset delete 1240 1241 --yes   # deletes the assets and their files\n```\n\nHow deleting works:\n- **Refunds:** a job that is already running is **not** refunded when you delete it.\n- **Confirming:** in a terminal you are asked first. Without one, `delete` needs `--yes`.\n- **Several ids:** each is deleted on its own. If some fail, the rest are still deleted, the failures are listed in `failed` with the reason, and the exit code is 1.\n\n## Importing your own models\n\n```bash\nzetta asset import ./chair.glb --title \"Lounge chair\"        # one file, stored as it is\nzetta asset import ./chair.obj ./chair.mtl ./wood.png        # a model and its files, zipped together\nzetta asset import ./chair-folder/                           # a whole folder\nzetta asset import ./chair.zip                               # a zip of a model and its textures\nzetta asset import ./my-rig.fbx --source 1234 --rigged       # a rig you made yourself from #1234\nzetta refine retopo <new id> --faces 5000 --yes              # then use any finishing tool on it\n```\n\nFormats: GLB, glTF, FBX, OBJ, STL, PLY, DAE, 3DS, 3MF, AMF, USD, USDA, USDC, USDZ, VRML (`.wrl`), LWO and KMZ, or a `.zip` of a model and its files.\n\nHow importing works:\n- **Free:** importing costs nothing.\n- **Nothing is converted.** One file is stored exactly as it is.\n- **Several files or a folder** are stored together as one zip, as the Studio does: an OBJ with its `.mtl` and textures, or a glTF with its `.bin` and images. A folder's files keep their paths inside it. When there are several models, the most complete format is the model (GLB, then glTF, FBX, OBJ …).\n- **Size:** up to 512 MB. Large files are uploaded in pieces, and retried if the connection drops.\n- **`--title`** names it in the library (default: the model's file name).\n- **`--source <id>`** records the library asset the model was made from. **`--rigged`** (needs `--source`) files it as a rig rather than an upload.\n\n## Saving back\n\n`zetta asset replace` saves a model file over an asset's stored GLB or FBX, as the Studio's Save does.\n\n```bash\nzetta asset replace 1234 ./chair-fixed.glb            # over #1234's GLB\nzetta asset replace 1240 ./part-2.glb --part 2 --yes  # one part of a segmented asset\nzetta asset replace 1250 ./rig.fbx --format fbx --yes # over the asset's FBX (a rig, a retopology)\n```\n\nSome details:\n- **It can't be undone.** The file is overwritten in place, and the old one is gone. The asset keeps its id, title and share link. In a terminal you are asked first; scripts and agents pass `--yes`.\n- **Parts** count from 1. A segmented asset needs `--part`, and parts are GLBs.\n- **Format:** your file decides which stored file is replaced: a GLB replaces the GLB, and an FBX the asset's one FBX. `--format glb|fbx`, if given, must match the file. Files can be up to 512 MB.\n\n## AI texture maps (paid plans)\n\n`zetta asset texture-map` makes PBR maps from a model's base-colour texture with AI: normal, roughness, metalness, emissive and ambient occlusion.\n\n```bash\nzetta asset texture-map 1234 --map normal --out ./maps                # just the pictures\nzetta asset texture-map 1234 --map roughness,metalness --apply        # put them on #1234 and save it\nzetta asset texture-map ./chair.glb --map normal --map ao --apply     # writes chair-maps.glb\nzetta asset texture-map ./albedo.png --map normal                     # from a base-colour picture\n```\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `--map normal\\|roughness\\|metalness\\|emissive\\|ao` | Which maps. Repeat the flag, or separate them with commas |\n| `--material <number or name>` | Only this material (numbers start at 0). Default: every material with a base-colour texture |\n| `--part <n>` | For a segmented asset: which part (1 = the first) |\n| `--out <folder>` | Where to save the maps (default: the current folder, or the `download.dir` setting) |\n| `--apply` | Also put the maps on the model and save it: over the asset's GLB (can't be undone), or for a local file as `<name>-maps.glb` in the output folder |\n\nHow it works:\n- **Source:** an asset id, a local `.glb`, or a base-colour picture (PNG, JPEG or WebP). `--apply` needs a model.\n- **Each map** is drawn by AI from the material's base-colour texture: square, at the texture's own size (1K, 2K or 4K). The maps are saved as pictures, and kept in your image library.\n- **Roughness and metalness** share one packed texture in a GLB. When the model's existing metal/rough texture is a JPEG, make both maps together (`--map roughness,metalness`); otherwise `--apply` refuses.\n- **Factors:** with `--apply`, a metallic or roughness factor of 0 is set to 1, and a black emissive colour to white, so the new map shows. The result lists each change as a note.\n- **Failures:** if some maps fail, the others are still saved, the failures are listed, and the exit code is 1.\n\n## Exporting\n\n`zetta asset export <id|file.glb> --format obj|fbx|stl` converts locally and for free. See [refining.md](refining.md#conversion).\n",
  "images.md": "# Images\n\nImage Studio from the terminal. Use it to make reference pictures for 3D, edit and combine pictures, and remove backgrounds. The results are saved as files, and also kept in your image library as `image:<id>`, which any command that takes a picture accepts.\n\nMaking, editing and combining pictures needs a paid plan. Without one, they fail with `PLAN_REQUIRED` (exit code 4).\n\n## Generate\n\n```bash\nzetta image generate -p \"a red vintage armchair, studio light, plain background\" --for-3d --out ./refs\n```\n\n| Flag | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `-p, --prompt` | What to draw |\n| `--for-3d` | A clean, square 1K picture made to be turned into 3D. It sets the aspect ratio, resolution and engine itself, so it can't go with `--engine` |\n| `--subject object\\|environment` | With `--for-3d`: an object on a plain background (default), or a whole environment |\n| `--aspect-ratio 1:1\\|16:9\\|…` | `9:16`, `2:3`, `3:4`, `4:5`, `1:1`, `5:4`, `4:3`, `3:2`, `16:9`, `21:9` |\n| `--resolution 1K\\|2K\\|4K` | 4K needs the `pro` engine and a paid plan. With `--engine`, a resolution that engine can't make is refused before anything is sent |\n| `--engine flash\\|pro` | Image engine by name (default: the best your plan allows). Some accounts also have `image-1`, `image-2` …; list them with `zetta catalog engines --images` |\n| `--fast` | Prefer the faster engine (an explicit `--engine` wins) |\n| `--style <file>` (up to 3) | Style reference pictures |\n| `--out <folder>` | Where to save (default: here, or the `download.dir` setting) |\n| `--no-download` | Keep the picture in your image library only (also: `zetta config set download.auto false`) |\n\n## Edit\n\n```bash\nzetta image edit ./chair.png -p \"make it dark green velvet\" --out ./refs\nzetta image edit image:812 -p \"remove the cushion\" --for-3d\n```\n\n## Combine 2–14 pictures\n\nThe pictures can be files, URLs or `image:<id>`, in any mix: at least 2 and at most 14 in all.\n\n```bash\nzetta image compose sofa.png lamp.png rug.png -p \"a cosy living room with these items\" --aspect-ratio 16:9\nzetta image compose image:812 image:813 ./logo.png -p \"the logo on both mugs\"\n```\n\n`edit` and `compose` take the same `--aspect-ratio`, `--resolution`, `--engine`, `--for-3d`, `--subject`, `--fast`, `--out` and `--no-download` flags as `generate`.\n\n## Remove a background\n\n```bash\nzetta image remove-bg photo.jpg                   # saves photo-nobg.png next to it\nzetta image remove-bg photo.jpg --out clean.png\n```\n\n`zetta generate --remove-bg` does this automatically before building a model.\n\n## The image library\n\n```bash\nzetta image list --kind generate --search lamp        # --kind generate, edit or compose\nzetta image get 812\nzetta image download 812 --out ./refs\nzetta image delete 812 --yes\n```\n\n## Pictures → 3D\n\n```bash\nzetta image generate -p \"a brass desk lamp\" --for-3d --out ./refs --json     # note the imageId\nzetta generate -i image:812 --engine n2.5 --max-credits 100 --out ./models\n```\n",
  "account.md": "# Authentication\n\n`zetta` uses your Zetta 3D account: the same library, credits and plan as the website. An account is connected through the browser: you sign in on zetta3d.ai and approve the CLI. There are two credentials:\n\n| Credential | Comes from | Lasts | Can do |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Browser sign-in** | `zetta auth login` (the installer starts it for you) | 30 days, then sign in again | Everything |\n| **API key** (`zk_live_…`) | `zetta apikey create`, or the website | Until you revoke it | Generating, refining, pictures, your library, pipeline runs and your balance. Not key management, billing or the account itself ([details](#what-an-api-key-can-do)) |\n\n## Signing in\n\n### With the browser (default)\n\nThe installer starts this for you right after installing, when you install in a terminal. Any time later:\n\n```bash\nzetta auth login\n```\n\nHow it works:\n1. `zetta` starts a one-time listener on `127.0.0.1`.\n2. It opens `zetta3d.ai/plugin-auth`. If you aren't signed in to the website, sign in there first.\n3. You click **Connect Zetta CLI**.\n4. The page hands a token to the listener. Only zetta3d.ai can do this, and only with a random secret that `zetta` generated for this sign-in.\n5. The listener closes.\n\nIf a browser can't open automatically, `zetta` prints the address. Open it on the same computer. `--print-url` always prints it instead of opening a browser.\n\n### With an API key: CI, scripts, servers, AI agents\n\nThe browser hands the sign-in to `127.0.0.1` on the same computer, so it can't finish over SSH, in a container or in CI. There, use an API key. On such a machine, `zetta auth login` says so instead of waiting.\n\nMake a key once, on the website (Profile › API keys) or on a machine where you're signed in with the browser:\n\n```bash\nzetta apikey create ci          # prints the key once; store it as a secret\n```\n\nThen, wherever zetta runs:\n\n```bash\nexport ZETTA_API_KEY=zk_live_…                        # nothing is stored; the variable wins over everything else\n# or save it as this profile's sign-in:\necho \"$ZETTA_API_KEY\" | zetta auth login --api-key\nzetta auth login --api-key                            # in a terminal: paste it at a hidden prompt\n```\n\n`--api-key` never takes the key as an argument, so it stays out of your shell history. A key starts with `zk_live_` and has 51 characters.\n\n## API keys\n\nManaging keys needs the browser sign-in: an API key cannot make, list or revoke keys. Making a key also needs a paid plan.\n\n```bash\nzetta apikey create ci                    # the key is shown once, never again\nzetta apikey create laptop --save         # saves it as this profile's sign-in instead of printing it\nzetta apikey list                         # id, name, hint (zk_live_Ab3x…wxyz), created and last used\nzetta apikey revoke 12                    # asks first; --yes without a terminal\nzetta apikey usage --key ci --days 7      # requests, errors, jobs, credits and AI tokens\nzetta apikey logs --errors --limit 100    # requests made with your keys, newest first\n```\n\n| Command | Options |\n|---|---|\n| `apikey create <name>` | The name says what the key is for, at most 60 characters. `--save` stores the key as this profile's sign-in (in the system keychain) and does not print it |\n| `apikey revoke <id>` | `--yes` revokes without asking. Anything using the key stops working at once |\n| `apikey usage` | `--key <id or name>` for one key (default: every key), `--days 1–90` (default 30) |\n| `apikey logs` | `--key <id or name>`, `--errors` (status 400 and up), `--limit 1–100` (default 50), `--before <id>` for the next page. Requests are kept 90 days |\n\nSome details:\n- **Limit:** an account can have 10 keys. Revoke one you no longer use to make room.\n- **`--save`** replaces the profile's browser sign-in. After it, managing keys and billing need `zetta auth login` again.\n- **Not an MCP tool:** `apikey create` is left out of `zetta mcp`, so a new key never lands in an agent's context.\n- **On the website:** keys are also under Profile › API keys.\n\n### What an API key can do\n\n| With an API key | Only with the browser sign-in |\n|---|---|\n| Check the account and plan: `auth status`, `account show`, `account credits`, `catalog …` | Managing API keys: `apikey …` |\n| Generate and refine: `generate`, `refine …` | Buying and billing changes: `billing checkout`, `billing agent-plan`, `billing portal` |\n| Your 3D library: `asset …`, including AI tags and rig checks | Trial codes: `account redeem` |\n| Pictures and background removal: `image …` | `account connections`, `account onboarding`, `account early-access` |\n| Pipelines: `pipeline list`, `get`, `estimate`, `run`, `batch`, and run control (`run …`) | Deleting the account |\n| Subscription and usage: `billing subscription`, `account usage` | Creating or changing pipelines: `pipeline create`, `update`, `duplicate`, `delete`, `draft` |\n| | Splats and worlds (`splat …`), and video uploads |\n\nWhat happens with a key:\n- **Commands that need a browser sign-in** fail at once, before any request, with `API_KEY_NOT_ALLOWED` (exit code 3). Run `zetta auth login`, and unset `ZETTA_API_KEY`.\n- **Pipeline changes** are refused with `PIPELINE_EDIT_NOT_ALLOWED`, and splats with `SPLAT_NOT_IN_API` (both exit code 4).\n- **Image engines** go by their public names (`flash`, `pro`, `image-1` …), as they do with a session.\n- **Rate limits:** API keys allow 300 requests a minute and 5,000 an hour. When the server asks to wait a minute or less, the CLI waits and tries again on its own. A longer wait fails with `RATE_LIMITED` (exit code 8), with the seconds to wait in `details.retryAfter`.\n\n## Checking and signing out\n\n```bash\nzetta auth status\n```\n\n```\nSigned in as you@example.com (creator plan) · profile default · session token from keychain · expires in 27 days\nSigned in as you@example.com (creator plan) · profile default · API key zk_live_Ab3x…wxyz from ZETTA_API_KEY · does not expire\n```\n\nWhat `auth status` tells you:\n- **The credential:** the browser sign-in (shown as a session token) or an API key, and where it comes from. A key is shown as a hint, never in full.\n- **Expiry:** when fewer than 3 days are left on the browser sign-in, it warns you. Once it expires, commands fail with `INVALID_SESSION` (exit code 3). Run `zetta auth login` again.\n- **A refused key:** a revoked or mistyped key fails with `INVALID_API_KEY` (exit code 3).\n\n```bash\nzetta auth logout         # forgets the saved sign-in (token or key) for this profile\n```\n\nOnly the local copy is deleted. An API key keeps working until you revoke it with `zetta apikey revoke <id>` (after `zetta auth login`) or on the website. If `ZETTA_API_KEY` is still set, `logout` says so.\n\n## Where the sign-in is kept\n\n| Order | Source | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `ZETTA_API_KEY` environment variable | An API key only; never written anywhere. Anything else there is refused (`INVALID_API_KEY`) |\n| 2 | System keychain | macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, or libsecret on Linux desktops |\n| 3 | `credentials.json` in the config folder, mode `0600` | Used when there is no keychain, or with `ZETTA_NO_KEYCHAIN=1` |\n\nHow it works:\n- **One saved sign-in per profile.** Saving an API key replaces a browser sign-in, and signing in with a browser replaces a saved key.\n- **`ZETTA_API_KEY` wins** over the saved sign-in, and is never saved. `auth login` warns you when it is set.\n- **`ZETTA_TOKEN` is no longer read.** Older versions took a pasted session token from it. If it is set and you aren't signed in, the error says so.\n\nThe config folder is `~/.config/zetta3d` (Windows: `%APPDATA%\\zetta3d`), or `ZETTA_CONFIG_DIR`. `zetta doctor` tells you which store is in use.\n\n## Security notes\n\n- **Accounts connect only through the browser.** You sign in on zetta3d.ai and approve the CLI there. The CLI never prints its session, and it can't be pasted into another machine; other machines use an API key.\n- **API keys come from a signed-in account.** They are made on the website or with `zetta apikey create` after the browser sign-in, and each one can be revoked.\n- **An API key works until you revoke it.** If one leaks, revoke it at once with `zetta apikey revoke <id>`. Use one key per machine or job, so you can revoke one without stopping the rest.\n- **The CLI never sees card details.** `zetta billing checkout` and `zetta billing portal` give you a link to the hosted payment page.\n\n## Your plan and billing\n\nThere is no free tier. Every command that spends needs a plan: 3D models, finishing tools, pictures, AI tags and rig checks, pipeline runs and drafts. Without one, they fail with `PLAN_REQUIRED` (exit code 4) and nothing is spent. A plan comes from a checkout, or from a trial code:\n\n```bash\nzetta billing plans                       # 3D plans, credit packs and Agent plans\nzetta billing checkout creator            # a link to pay for a plan (--yearly to pay yearly)\nzetta account redeem ZETTA-7KQ2-M9XD      # a trial code: 10 days of the Basic plan\n```\n\nA trial code works once per account, on an account without a plan. It takes any form: `ZETTA-7KQ2-M9XD`, lower case, without dashes, or the whole link with `?trial=`.\n\n### Your account\n\n```bash\nzetta account show                        # email, plan, trial, Agent plan, credits and access\nzetta account credits                     # balances per pool\nzetta account usage --meter ai-tokens     # what was charged and refunded, and where\nzetta account usage --breakdown           # this period's totals by feature\nzetta account connections                 # plugins and CLIs connected to the account\n```\n\nSome details:\n- **`account show`** has `trial` (the plan and end date while a trial runs), `trialAvailable` (a trial code can still be redeemed) and `agentPlan` (an Agent plan is in force).\n- **`account credits`** has `canSpend`, which is `false` without a plan.\n- **`account usage`** lists entries newest first. Each says where it was spent (`origin`: web, unity, blender, unreal, pipeline, cli or api) and, for AI turns, the tier. `--meter credits|ai-tokens` shows one balance.\n- **`--breakdown`** sums this billing period (the last 30 days without a subscription) by feature, and AI tokens also by tier. It takes only `--meter` (default `credits`).\n- **`account connections`** lists each plugin and CLI with its version, its host app's version, and when it was first and last seen.\n- **`account onboarding`** answers the five onboarding questions. Only some plans pay a bonus for it; `account show` says whether one is waiting.\n\n### Billing\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `billing plans` | 3D plans, credit packs, and Agent plans (AI tokens for the agents, every month) |\n| `billing checkout <plan> [--yearly]` | A link to buy a 3D plan |\n| `billing checkout --pack <credits>` | A link to buy a credit pack, on top of a plan |\n| `billing checkout --agent <name>` | A link to buy an Agent plan. Agent plans are monthly only, so no `--yearly`. An account has one at a time |\n| `billing agent-plan <name>` | Switch your Agent plan to another size. The prorated difference is charged to your card at once (a smaller plan is credited). Asks first; `--yes` without a terminal |\n| `billing subscription` | Your plan and Agent plan: status and renewal dates |\n| `billing portal` | A link to manage your subscription, Agent plan, card and invoices |\n\nSome details:\n- **`billing agent-plan`** is not an MCP tool, because it charges a card. A declined card changes nothing.\n- **Deleting the account** (`zetta account delete`) is refused with `SUBSCRIPTION_ACTIVE` while a plan or an Agent plan is live. Cancel both first with `zetta billing portal`.\n\n## Profiles: several accounts or servers\n\nEach profile has its own API address and its own sign-in.\n\n```bash\nzetta config profiles                                   # default, local, and any you added\nzetta --profile local auth login                        # sign in to a local development server\nzetta config add-profile staging --api https://staging-api.example.com/api --site https://staging.example.com\nzetta config use staging                                # make it the default\nZETTA_PROFILE=staging zetta asset list                  # or pick it per command\n```\n\n`local` is built in: it points at `http://localhost:4000/api` and `http://localhost:3000`.\n",
  "configuration.md": "# Configuration\n\n`zetta` works with no configuration. Everything below is optional.\n\n## Settings\n\n```bash\nzetta config list                  # every setting, its value and what it does\nzetta config get output\nzetta config set <key> <value>\nzetta config unset <key>\n```\n\n| Key | Values | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `output` | `text`, `json`, `ndjson` | Default output format. Otherwise: text in a terminal, JSON when piped |\n| `defaults.wait` | `true` / `false` | Paid commands wait for their job unless `--no-wait` is given |\n| `defaults.engine` | `n2.5`, `np1`, `np1.5` | Default engine for `zetta generate` (`n2.5` when unset). N1 is retired: a saved `n1` fails with a message saying so; set another engine or unset it |\n| `download.auto` | `true` / `false` | Download finished jobs into the folder you run `zetta` in. Unset: on in a terminal, off for scripts and agents. See [Automatic downloads](#automatic-downloads) |\n| `skills.auto` | `true` / `false` | Install the agent skill for Claude Code, Codex and Cursor automatically, and keep it current (default `true`). `zetta skill uninstall` sets it to `false`; `zetta skill install` back to `true` |\n| `download.dir` | a folder | Where downloads go instead of the current folder. A relative path starts from the current folder, e.g. `models` |\n| `download.formats` | formats, comma-separated | Only these formats in automatic downloads, e.g. `glb,fbx` (default: every result file) |\n| `spend.maxPerCommand` | a number | Credits one command may spend without `--yes`; anything pricier is refused. Lets scripts and agents spend without confirming each command |\n| `spend.requireConfirm` | `true` / `false` | Ask before spending in a terminal (default `true`) |\n| `currentProfile` | a profile name | The profile used when `--profile` isn't given |\n\n## Automatic downloads\n\nWhen a generation, a finishing job or a pipeline run is done, `zetta` downloads the results into the folder you ran it in. You don't need `--out`.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/projects/game/assets\nzetta generate -i chair.png --engine n2.5 --max-credits 60   # waits, then saves 1234-model.glb here\n```\n\n**Turning it on or off:**\n\n```bash\nzetta config set download.auto false   # off: jobs return at once with their id\nzetta config set download.auto true    # on everywhere, scripts and agents too\nzetta config unset download.auto       # back to the default: on in a terminal only\n```\n\nFor one command, `--download` turns it on and `--no-download` turns it off. `--no-wait` also skips it, because the command returns before the job is done.\n\n**What gets downloaded:**\n\n| Command | Downloads |\n|---|---|\n| `generate`, `refine …`, `splat …` | Every result file of the new asset |\n| `asset wait <id>` | Every result file of that asset |\n| `pipeline run`, `pipeline batch`, `run wait` | The final models of each run, the last step of every branch (`run outputs <id>` gets every step) |\n| `image generate`, `image edit`, `image compose` | The pictures. They come back with the reply, so they save even for scripts. Only `download.auto false` or `--no-download` keeps them in your image library alone |\n\n**Details:**\n- **Where:** files go into the current folder, or `download.dir`. They are named `<id>-<file>`, e.g. `1234-model.glb`, and existing files are never overwritten.\n- **Formats:** `download.formats glb,fbx` keeps only those. A command's own `--format` wins over it.\n- **Failures:** an automatic download never fails a finished job. If a file can't be downloaded, or none matches your formats, you get a warning and the command to download it later.\n- **`--out <folder>`** works as before: it waits and downloads into that folder, whatever the setting.\n- **Scripts and agents** get the same JSON as with `--out`. The saved paths are in `files` (or `saved`).\n- **MCP tools** never download on their own, because the server's folder isn't yours. A tool call downloads only when it passes `download: true` or `out`.\n\nSettings live in `config.json` in the config folder: `~/.config/zetta3d` (or `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zetta3d`). On Windows it is `%APPDATA%\\zetta3d`. `ZETTA_CONFIG_DIR` overrides it.\n\n## Profiles\n\nA profile is an API address plus its own sign-in.\n\n```bash\nzetta config profiles\nzetta config add-profile staging --api https://staging-api.example.com/api --site https://staging.example.com --use\nzetta config use default\nzetta config remove-profile staging\nzetta --profile staging asset list\n```\n\n`zetta config profiles` shows the account each profile's saved sign-in is for. It shows saved sign-ins only: `ZETTA_API_KEY` belongs to no single profile.\n\n| Built-in | API | Website |\n|---|---|---|\n| `default` | `https://api.zetta3d.ai/api` | `https://zetta3d.ai` |\n| `local` | `http://localhost:4000/api` | `http://localhost:3000` |\n\n## Global flags\n\nThese work with every command:\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `--json`, `-o, --output text\\|json\\|ndjson` | Output format |\n| `--profile <name>` | Use another profile |\n| `--api-url <url>` | Override the API address for this command |\n| `--verbose` | Show each API request on stderr |\n| `-q, --quiet` | Print only the result |\n| `--no-color` | No colours |\n| `--non-interactive` | Never prompt; fail instead (implied in CI) |\n| `--input <json\\|@file\\|->` | Give the command's inputs as one JSON object |\n| `-v, --version`, `-h, --help` | |\n\n## Environment variables\n\n| Variable | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `ZETTA_API_KEY` | Use this API key. It wins over everything else and is never stored |\n| `ZETTA_PROFILE` | Profile to use |\n| `ZETTA_API_URL`, `ZETTA_SITE_URL` | Override the profile's addresses |\n| `ZETTA_OUTPUT` | `text`, `json` or `ndjson` |\n| `ZETTA_CONFIG_DIR` | Where settings and the saved token or key live |\n| `ZETTA_NO_KEYCHAIN=1` | Keep the saved token or key in the `0600` file, not the system keychain |\n| `ZETTA_NON_INTERACTIVE=1` | Never prompt |\n| `ZETTA_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1` | No new-version check or notice (normally at most once an hour, at a terminal) |\n| `ZETTA_NO_LOGIN=1` | The installers don't start the browser sign-in after installing |\n| `ZETTA_NO_SKILL=1` | Don't install or refresh the agent skill automatically (installers, upgrades) |\n| `ZETTA_DOWNLOAD_BASE` | Download host for `zetta upgrade` (and the installers) |\n| `NO_COLOR` | No colours |\n| `CI=true` | Implies no prompts |\n\n## Output formats\n\n| Format | Result | Progress | Errors |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `text` (terminals) | Readable text on stdout | One status line on stderr | `Error: …` and a hint on stderr |\n| `json` (when piped) | One JSON document on stdout | None | One JSON line on stderr |\n| `ndjson` | `{\"type\":\"result\",\"data\":…}` on stdout | `{\"type\":\"progress\",…}` lines on stdout | `{\"type\":\"error\",…}` on stdout |\n\n## Shell completion\n\n```bash\nzetta completion zsh  > ~/.zfunc/_zetta        # then: fpath+=~/.zfunc; autoload -U compinit; compinit\nzetta completion bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/zetta\nzetta completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/zetta.fish\n```\n",
  "errors.md": "# Troubleshooting\n\nStart with:\n\n```bash\nzetta doctor          # version, config, connection, clock, sign-in, keychain\nzetta --verbose …     # shows every API request on stderr\n```\n\nEvery error names a code, and most give a hint with the next command to run. As JSON:\n\n```json\n{\"error\":{\"code\":\"…\",\"message\":\"…\",\"details\":{…},\"hint\":\"…\",\"exitCode\":N}}\n```\n\n## By exit code\n\n### 2: fix the input\n\n| Code | Meaning | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `USAGE` | A flag or argument is wrong or missing | `zetta <command> --help`; `details.problems` lists each issue |\n| `BUILD_PROBLEMS` | These options don't work together, for example `--parts` on np1 | Follow the message; `zetta catalog engines` shows each engine's options |\n| `VALIDATION_FAILED` | The server rejected a field | `details.problems` |\n| `FILE_NOT_FOUND`, `UNSUPPORTED_FILE_TYPE` | The file is missing, or of a type the command doesn't take (pictures: PNG, JPEG or WebP) | Check the path and type |\n| `INVALID_IMAGE`, `FILE_TOO_LARGE` | Pictures must be 128–5000 px and at most 8 MB | Resize or compress the picture |\n| `PIPELINE_NOT_RUNNABLE` | The recipe has problems | `zetta pipeline validate <recipe>` |\n| `RUN_NEEDS_INPUT` | A run needs a per-run input (for example a texture prompt) | `--draft`, then `zetta run set-input` |\n| `MODEL_CAPABILITY_UNSUPPORTED` | That engine can't do that option | Pick another engine |\n| `IMAGE_GEN_REJECTED` | The image engine turned the request down | Rephrase the prompt or use another picture, then try again |\n| `API_KEY_NAME_REQUIRED`, `API_KEY_NAME_TOO_LONG` | An API key needs a name of at most 60 characters | `zetta apikey create <name>` |\n| `UNKNOWN_PRICE` | There is no such plan, pack or price to buy | `zetta billing plans` |\n\n### 3: sign in\n\n| Code | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| `NOT_LOGGED_IN` | `zetta auth login`, or set `ZETTA_API_KEY` |\n| `INVALID_SESSION` | The session token expired (after 30 days) or was refused. Run `zetta auth login` again |\n| `INVALID_API_KEY` | The API key is mistyped or was revoked. Make a new one with `zetta apikey create <name>` (after `zetta auth login`), or on the website under Profile › API keys |\n| `API_KEY_NOT_ALLOWED` | The command needs the browser sign-in, and this profile uses an API key. Run `zetta auth login`, and unset `ZETTA_API_KEY`. See [what an API key can do](account.md#what-an-api-key-can-do) |\n| `LOGIN_TIMEOUT` | The browser sign-in wasn't finished within 10 minutes. Run `zetta auth login` again |\n\nThe browser doesn't come back to the terminal?\n- **Same computer:** the sign-in page must be opened on the computer running `zetta`.\n- **Over SSH, in a container or on a server:** the browser can't reach this computer. Use an API key instead (`ZETTA_API_KEY`).\n- **Strict browser settings** can block the page from reaching `127.0.0.1`. Allow it, or use an API key.\n\n### 4: your plan or access\n\n| Code | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| `PLAN_REQUIRED` | There is no plan, so nothing can be generated. See `zetta billing plans` and subscribe with `zetta billing checkout <plan>`, or redeem a trial code with `zetta account redeem <code>` |\n| `PLAN_CAPABILITY_REQUIRED` | `zetta catalog entitlements` shows what your plan allows; `zetta billing plans` |\n| `EARLY_ACCESS_REQUIRED` | `zetta account early-access \"<feature>\"` |\n| `IMAGE_EDITING_REQUIRES_PLAN` | Image editing and AI texture maps need a paid plan: `zetta billing plans`, or a trial code with `zetta account redeem <code>` |\n| `IMAGE_4K_REQUIRES_PLAN`, `IMAGE_PRO_MODEL_REQUIRES_PLAN` | Use `--resolution 2K`, or the default image engine, or upgrade |\n| `SPLAT_NOT_IN_API` | Splats, worlds and video uploads don't work with an API key. Sign in with `zetta auth login`, or make them on the website |\n| `PIPELINE_EDIT_NOT_ALLOWED` | With an API key, pipelines can be listed, estimated and run, but not created or changed. Run a saved pipeline by id, or sign in with `zetta auth login` to edit it |\n| `AI_ADD_ON_NEEDS_ACCOUNT` | Confirm your email first (finish signing in once on the website), then try again |\n\n### 5: credits and the spend guard\n\n| Code | Meaning | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SPEND_NOT_CONFIRMED` | A paid command ran without a terminal and without `--yes` or `--max-credits` | Add `--max-credits N` (or `--yes`); check the price with `--dry-run` |\n| `SPEND_LIMIT` | The price is over `--max-credits`, the configured limit, or the MCP session budget | `details.credits` is the price |\n| `INSUFFICIENT_CREDITS` | Not enough credits | `zetta account credits`; `zetta billing checkout --pack 1000` |\n\n### 6 to 11\n\n| Exit | Code | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6 | `NOT_FOUND` | Wrong id, or it belongs to another account |\n| 6 | `API_KEY_NOT_FOUND` | No such API key. `zetta apikey list` shows your keys and their ids |\n| 7 | `JOB_LIMIT_REACHED` | Your plan's concurrent-job limit. Wait for a job: `zetta asset wait <id>` |\n| 7 | `GEN3D_NOT_READY` | The source asset is still processing. Wait for it first |\n| 7 | `SUBSCRIPTION_ACTIVE` | Cancel the subscription and any Agent plan before deleting the account: `zetta billing portal`. Check both with `zetta billing subscription` |\n| 7 | `API_KEY_LIMIT` | An account can have 10 API keys. Revoke one you no longer use: `zetta apikey list`, then `zetta apikey revoke <id>` |\n| 7 | `AI_ADD_ON_ACTIVE` | You already have an Agent plan. Change its size with `zetta billing agent-plan <name>` |\n| 7 | `NO_AI_ADD_ON` | There is no Agent plan to change. Buy one with `zetta billing checkout --agent <name>` |\n| 7 | `SAME_AI_ADD_ON` | That is already your Agent plan. `zetta billing plans` shows the sizes |\n| 7 | `NO_CUSTOMER` | There is nothing to manage yet. Buy a plan with `zetta billing checkout <plan>` |\n| 8 | `RATE_LIMITED` | Too many requests. Wait the seconds in `details.retryAfter`. API keys allow 300 requests a minute and 5,000 an hour. The CLI itself waits out any wait of up to a minute. For many jobs at once, use a pipeline batch |\n| 9 | `JOB_FAILED`, `RUN_FAILED` | The job failed and was refunded. The message says why. Try a cleaner picture (`--remove-bg`), other options or another engine |\n| 9 | `IMAGE_GEN_NO_IMAGE` | No picture came back. Try again; if it keeps happening, change the prompt or the picture |\n| 10 | `WAIT_TIMEOUT` | Not an error: the job is still running. Run `zetta asset wait <id>` (or `zetta run wait <id>`) again |\n| 11 | `NETWORK_ERROR`, `REQUEST_TIMEOUT` | Check your connection or proxy; `zetta doctor` |\n| 11 | `SERVER_ERROR`, `FEATURE_UNAVAILABLE` | A temporary problem, or the feature is switched off. Try later |\n\n## Other problems\n\n| Problem | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| `zetta: command not found` | Open a new terminal, or `export PATH=\"$HOME/.zetta/bin:$PATH\"` |\n| `doctor` says the clock is off | Fix the system clock (sign-in tokens depend on it) |\n| `doctor` says \"no system keychain\" | Normal on servers and in containers: the saved token or key is kept in a `0600` file instead |\n| Downloaded models look wrong in an app | Try another format (`--format fbx`), or convert locally (`zetta asset export --format …`) |\n| Local export says the model uses compressed geometry | Convert on the server: `zetta refine convert <id> --to fbx` |\n| Alpine: the binary won't start | `apk add libstdc++` |\n| Output contains colour codes in logs | `--no-color` or `NO_COLOR=1` (colour is already off when output isn't a terminal) |\n| Piping into `head` | Supported: `zetta … \\| head` exits quietly |\n\nStill stuck? Run the failing command with `--verbose --json` and send the output to Zetta 3D support. It contains no token or key.\n",
}
